      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 American College of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Avoidance strategies in patients with cow's milk allergy occasionally fail to protect these patients from inadvertent exposures, leading to life-threatening reactions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess the safety and efficacy of milk oral immunotherapy as an alternative therapeutic strategy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Patients (n = 280, >4 years old) with IgE-mediated cow's milk allergy were enrolled into a milk oral immunotherapy program at a single hospital center. High-risk patients were not excluded. The treatment protocol consisted of 3 rounds of oral induction performed every 4 weeks. On day 1, a patient's reaction threshold was determined. On days 2 and 3, a tolerated starting dose below the threshold was confirmed. Day 4 mimicked the home treatment, which continued until the next induction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The median initial starting dose was 52.5 mg of cow's milk protein. Excluding those whose treatment failed in the first week (n = 5) or are still undergoing treatment (n = 15), 61.5% (160 of 260 patients) achieved 7,200 mg and 85.4% of patients were consuming at least 180 mg of milk protein. Reactions at home requiring the use of injectable epinephrine occurred in 15.7% of patients (44 of 280) and in 0.075% (58 of 77,098) of doses administered. Predictors for achieving a full dose in multivariate analysis included a starting dose higher than 30 mg of milk protein (odds ratio 4.6, P < .001), not requiring epinephrine during induction (odds ratio 5.2, P < .001) or home treatment (odds ratio 2.6, P = .037), and the lack of nonanaphylactic type symptoms (odds ratio 15.6, P < .001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Milk oral immunotherapy, carried out in a highly controlled setting, is successful in protecting the overwhelming majority of patients from accidental exposures to cow's milk protein."
          }
        ]
      },
